[Music]  hi lisa hi vivek  hi stephen hi lisa i'm really excited to  talk to you both today about this  lisa tell me a bit about the hair has no  gender project  well this is really part of pantene's  journey to  bring everyone more great hair days and  hair has no gender is really about  telling  and amplifying the stories and the  voices of our transgender  and gender non-binary communities to  really show the power  that hair has in expressing um you know  personal identity and self-expression  and really then calling on all canadians  to be vocal  allies we believe that gender is not  distinguished by hair  and that hair is not defined by gender  your hair is fantastic vivid  i love it tell me what role would you  say  your hair plays in your own  self-expression  yeah so i mean when pantene approached  me i was super excited because i feel  like hair is such a big part of who i am  as an  individual i think starting as a child  i learned that hair was kind of an  inexpensive way  to express your individuality and your  identity and i think as  my identities have evolved so has my  hair you know i've been growing this out  i was counting today i think seven years  and i feel like i'm a different person  now than i was seven years ago so  i think that hair is such an important  part  of our community's stories and the way  that we express ourselves  i love the idea of recreating family  photos with the ambassadors this film  series is  when i was watching it it was emotional  for me it was thought provoking  it was educational vivek how did it feel  to go  back with your dad and recreate a photo  like this because this is something  you're going to look back  on and be able to smile at in many years  to come  i feel like i have this like keepsake  that i can always look back on and be  like wow we  found a way to not only you know  recreate this photo but also  reconnect in a way that i feel like  we're still trying to do  um in our older ages obviously i was  moved sitting next to my dad but i also  felt kind of nervous because my dad and  i you know our relationship  is kind of broad and so this is a very  new thing for us to do together to sit  together and talk about  feelings and history but watching the  film back i was like oh wow like  look at the way he looks at me like i  was just i couldn't take my eyes  off of like the love in his eyes which  again i felt like  kind of deepened the message that i was  trying to hit in the film  that acceptance does have a range of  acceptance looks like a range of ways  and you know i think it for me watching  the film it's a reminder to actually  look in the eyes of the people who love  you because again sometimes they're not  saying it with words but they're saying  it with their eyes so yeah i was very  very very moved  i've had to really broaden my idea  of what acceptance looks like was it  christmas or diwali  no it's christmas listen you said in the  video  that you had to broaden your idea of  acceptance  and i think that's a very powerful  statement so tell me  what has that journey been like for you  i think as  you know a queer person as a trans  person when i turn on the tv and i see  stories of acceptance you always have  like  you know the mom at the pride ceremony  or you know  the the stubborn dad who at the end of  the show  will be like i love you son and you know  i never got those kinds of forms of  acceptance i think what's beautiful  about you know hair has no gender is  that it shows that  you know a acceptance is important and  crucial and that we need acceptance from  allies to be able to express ourselves  and have the confidence to express  ourselves  but also that acceptance can have many  forms so  i think you know spreading the word  about the films is a really great  starting point  oh hey there if you want more lgbtq news  and exclusive interviews  click subscribe or watch these videos

